==English==

===Etymology===
Uncertain origin, most likely Middle English compounds equivalent to {{compound|en|foot|hook}}.
 
A compound of {{inh|en|enm|fut}} and {{m|dum|hoek}}, {{cog|ang|fōt}} + {{m|ang|-oc}}, {{m|ang|-uc|tr=diminutive suffix}}, {{m|ang|hok}}, or {{m|enm|-ock}}, or {{m|dum|hok}}.

Similarity in {{inh|frr|ofs|fōt}}, {{cog|osx|fōt}} (Lower German (Niederdeutsch)), {{inh|af|odt|fuot}}, [[Alemannic]] German {{l|en|Alemannic|hocke}}

Potentially related to Middle Dutch {{m|dum|voet|}} + {{m|mdt|kijn}}, diminutive of {{m|dum|voet|t=foot}}, equivalent to {{suffix|en|foot|kin|nocat=1}}.
 
Also a previously suggested word was {{inh|en|enm|futtek}}.

Perhaps came into Olde English further back, from {{der|da|non|øx||ax}}, Danish & {{der|da|non|økse||axe}}, {{m|enm|futt økse}}, {{m|enm|futt oks}}, & {{m|non|fótr||foot}}, meaning {{m|enm|fet ax}}, {{m|enm|fett oeks}}, {{m|enm|fet okse}} in variations of Norwegian, equivalent to English {{compound|en|great|axe}}, {{compound|en|bold|axe}} or {{compound|en|fat|ox}}.

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|nautical}} Any of the [[curved]] [[timber]]s that form the [[rib]]s of a [[ship]].
#* {{quote-book|year=1884|author={{w|Dixon Kemp}}|title=A Manual of Yacht and Boat Sailing (Fourth Edition)|page=12|passage=The timbers (called also frames, or floors, first, second and third '''futtocks''', where the lengths of the frames are in two, three, or more pieces) will be "double," that is, two timbers will be placed close together, or nearly close together, and act as one frame.}}

